      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to consider the concept of circular permutations and constraints for specific subsets of people. The total number of ways to arrange nine people in a circular manner is given by \\((9-1)!\\) (since circular permutations are counted by \\((n-1)!\\)):\n\n\\[\n(9-1)! = 8!\n\\]\n\nNext, let's determine the number of favorable arrangements where all four math majors sit consecutively. To facilitate this, we can \"glue\" the four consecutive mathematicians together as a single \"block\" or \"super person\", reducing the problem to arranging 6 \"items\": the \"group\" of 4 math majors as one \"block\", plus the 3 physics majors and the two biology majors.\n\nThe number of ways to arrange these 6 \"items\" in a circle is:\n\n\\[\n(6-1)! = 5!\n\\]\n\nNow, within the \"block\" of the 4 math majors, there are 4! ways to arrange them among themselves since:\n\n\\[\n4! = 24\n\\]\n\nThus, the total number of favorable arrangements is:\n\n\\[\n(5!) \\cdot (4!) = 5! \\cdot 24\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability \\( P \\) that all four math majors sit consecutively is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\nP = \\frac{\\text{(Number of favorable arrangements)}}{\\text{(Total number of arrangements)}} = \\frac{5! \\cdot 24}{8!}\n\\]\n\nCalculating factorials:\n\n\\[\n5! = 120 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 8! = 40320\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nP = \\frac{120 \\cdot 24}{40320} = \\frac{2880}{40320} = \\frac{1}{14}\n\\]\n\nHence, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\n\\]",
