      "text": "To determine the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats at a round table with nine people, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Total number of seating arrangements:**\n   There are 9 people seated in a circle, so the total number of ways to arrange them is given by \\((9-1)! = 8!\\).\n\n   \\[\n   8! = 40320\n   \\]\n\n2. **Number of favorable arrangements (all math majors in consecutive seats):**\n   \n   - First, we treat the block of four consecutive math majors as a single \"super person\". This effectively reduces the problem to arranging 6 \"people\" (the super block and the other 5 individuals).\n   - In a circle, there are \\((6-1)!\\) ways to arrange these 6 \"people\". This is again calculated as:\n   \\[\n   5! = 120\n   \\]\n\n   Additionally, we need to consider the circular permutations of the four math majors within their block. So, there are \\(4!\\) ways to arrange 4 math majors in a circle:\n   \\[\n   4! = 24\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, the total number of favorable arrangements is:\n   \\[\n   5! \\times 4! = 120 \\times 24 = 2880\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculating the probability:**\n   The probability is the ratio of the number of favorable arrangements to the total number of arrangements:\n   \\[\n   P = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable arrangements}}{\\text{Total number of arrangements}} = \\frac{2880}{40320}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying this fraction:\n   \\[\n   P = \\frac{1}{14}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\n\\]",
